#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# edge_form

#% text
# `edge_form(d)` is a graphics directive: the edges of polygons, rectangles and disks that follow are drawn with the directive d, or with every directive of a list d.

#% text
# `edge_form()` and `edge_form(none)` draw no edges, the default.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The directives inside are colours, `opacity`, `thickness`, `absolute_thickness`, `dashing` and `absolute_dashing`; edges start black and 1 point wide.

#% item
# Edges do not change the current colour of what follows; the face is still filled as before.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A disk with a black edge:

#% cell
graphics([orange, edge_form(black), disk()])
#% output
#= image("edge_form.files/edge_form-1.svgz")

#% text
# A thick dashed red edge around an empty rectangle:

#% cell
graphics([face_form(none), edge_form([red, absolute_thickness(3), dashing(dashed)]), rectangle([0, 0], [2, 1])])
#% output
#= image("edge_form.files/edge_form-2.svgz")

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Only directives may appear inside:

#% cell
edge_form(7)
#% output
#! failure(argument, edge_form, 1)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `face_form`, `polygon`, `disk`, `rectangle`
